CM_ "UNIVERSAL OBD-II DBC for SavvyCAN

Compatible with virtually any OBD-II vehicle (1996+ in the US, 2001+ in Europe/Japan).
Standard CAN IDs:
  0x7DF (2015) — Functional broadcast diagnostic request (queries any responding ECU)
  0x7E0 (2016) — Physical request to ECU #1 (Engine — ECM)
  0x7E1 (2017) — Physical request to ECU #2 (Transmission — TCM)
  ...
  0x7E7 (2023) — Physical request to ECU #8
  0x7E8 (2024) — Response from ECU #1 (ECM)
  0x7E9 (2025) — Response from ECU #2 (TCM)
  ...
  0x7EF (2031) — Response from ECU #8

Format of any OBD-II request/response:
  Byte 0: ISO-TP single frame length (3 for query, 4-5 for response with 1-2 data bytes)
  Byte 1: Service ID
           0x01 = Show current data
           0x02 = Show freeze frame data
           0x03 = Show stored DTCs
           0x04 = Clear DTCs
           0x05 = Test results, oxygen sensor monitoring
           0x07 = Show pending DTCs
           0x09 = Vehicle info (VIN, etc.)
           0x0A = Permanent DTCs
           0x22 = ReadDataByIdentifier (extended UDS, manufacturer-specific DIDs)
           0x2E = WriteDataByIdentifier (extended UDS)
  Byte 2: PID (Parameter ID) for service 0x01, or first byte of DID for service 0x22
  Bytes 3-7: PID-specific data (or padding 0xAA in some implementations)

Response format (service = request_service + 0x40):
  Byte 0: ISO-TP length
  Byte 1: Service response (0x41 for current data, 0x62 for ReadDID, etc.)
  Byte 2: PID echo
  Bytes 3-7: Data (A, B, C, D, E variables in OBD-II spec)

This DBC defines the most common Service 01 PIDs as separate BO_ entries to make
SavvyCAN auto-decode them when received. For service 0x22 (manufacturer DIDs),
use vehicle-specific DBCs (e.g. vw_mk7_cluster.dbc).

To USE in SavvyCAN:
  1. File → DBC files → Manage → Load this DBC (and your vehicle DBC)
  2. Send a query: ID 0x7DF, length 8, data: '02 01 0C 00 00 00 00 00' (Service 01, PID 0x0C = RPM)
  3. Response will appear on 0x7E8: '04 41 0C XX XX 00 00 00' where XX XX = RPM*4 big-endian
  4. SavvyCAN will auto-decode it as 'Engine_RPM = N RPM' if this DBC is loaded.

Notes on encoding in this DBC:
  - Sender 'Tester' = OBD-II scanner / your tool
  - Sender 'ECM' = Engine Control Module (responds to 0x7E0 queries with 0x7E8)
  - Each PID response (0x7E8 with specific PID byte) is modeled as a separate BO_
    so signal positions and scaling can be auto-decoded by SavvyCAN.
  - PIDs are typed with byte order @0+ (big-endian) for multi-byte values per OBD-II spec.

Common PIDs covered:
  04 — Engine load %
  05 — Coolant temp
  0B — Intake manifold absolute pressure (kPa)
  0C — Engine RPM
  0D — Vehicle speed
  0E — Timing advance
  0F — Intake air temp
  10 — MAF airflow
  11 — Throttle position
  14 — O2 sensor B1S1
  21 — Distance with MIL on
  2F — Fuel level
  31 — Distance since DTC clear
  33 — Barometric pressure
  42 — Battery voltage
  43 — Absolute load value
  46 — Ambient air temperature
  51 — Fuel type
  5C — Engine oil temp
  5E — Engine fuel rate

Reference: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OBD-II_PIDs (full PID list)";


CM_ BO_ 2015 "Functional broadcast OBD-II request — any responding ECU will reply (typically 0x7E8 for ECM).";
CM_ BO_ 2024 "Physical query to Engine ECU (ECM). Responses come back on 0x7E8.";
CM_ BO_ 2032 "Generic ECM response container — for unknown PIDs, parse manually. Specific PIDs have dedicated BO_ entries below.";

CM_ BO_ 1280 "OBD-II PID 0x05 — Engine Coolant Temperature. 1 byte, formula: temp_C = A - 40, range -40 to 215 °C.";
CM_ BO_ 1281 "OBD-II PID 0x0C — Engine RPM. 2 bytes big-endian, formula: RPM = (256*A + B) / 4, range 0 to 16383.75 RPM.";
CM_ BO_ 1282 "OBD-II PID 0x0D — Vehicle Speed. 1 byte, formula: speed_kmh = A, range 0 to 255 km/h.";
CM_ BO_ 1283 "OBD-II PID 0x0F — Intake Air Temperature. 1 byte, formula: temp_C = A - 40.";
CM_ BO_ 1284 "OBD-II PID 0x10 — MAF (Mass Air Flow) sensor. 2 bytes BE, formula: g/s = (256*A + B) / 100.";
CM_ BO_ 1285 "OBD-II PID 0x11 — Throttle Position. 1 byte, formula: % = 100*A/255.";
CM_ BO_ 1286 "OBD-II PID 0x2F — Fuel Tank Level Input. 1 byte, formula: % = 100*A/255.";
CM_ BO_ 1287 "OBD-II PID 0x46 — Ambient air temperature. 1 byte, formula: temp_C = A - 40.";
CM_ BO_ 1288 "OBD-II PID 0x5C — Engine Oil Temperature. 1 byte, formula: temp_C = A - 40.";
CM_ BO_ 1289 "OBD-II PID 0x42 — Control module voltage (battery). 2 bytes BE, formula: V = (256*A + B) / 1000.";
CM_ BO_ 1290 "OBD-II PID 0x5E — Engine fuel rate. 2 bytes BE, formula: L/h = (256*A + B) * 0.05.";
CM_ BO_ 1291 "OBD-II PID 0x0B — Intake Manifold Absolute Pressure. 1 byte, formula: kPa = A. For boost in psi: ((A - 100) * 0.145).";
CM_ BO_ 1292 "OBD-II PID 0x04 — Calculated Engine Load. 1 byte, formula: % = 100*A/255.";
CM_ BO_ 1293 "OBD-II PID 0x0E — Timing Advance. 1 byte, formula: degrees = A/2 - 64.";
CM_ BO_ 1297 "OBD-II PID 0x33 — Absolute Barometric Pressure. 1 byte, formula: kPa = A.";
CM_ BO_ 1298 "OBD-II PID 0x51 — Fuel type. 1 byte enum.";
